---
title: OAuth
description: The NeoWorks authorization model — PKCE, scopes, refresh tokens, and offline JWT verification.
---

NeoWorks uses standard OAuth 2.0 with PKCE. Any compliant OAuth library works — there is
nothing proprietary in the flow.

## Scopes

Scopes are `resource:action` pairs. Request the minimum your app needs; users see exactly
what you ask for and can revoke access at any time.

| Scope            | Grants                                  |
| ---------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `contacts:read`  | Read names, emails, phone numbers       |
| `contacts:write` | Create and update contacts              |
| `tasks:read`     | Read task titles, due dates, status     |
| `tasks:write`    | Create, update and complete tasks       |
| `files:read`     | Read file metadata and content          |
| `files:write`    | Write files to the user's Grove         |

Scope enforcement happens in SurrealDB, not in middleware. A token can only ever touch
the rows and actions it was granted.

## Refresh tokens

Access tokens are short-lived (one hour). Use the refresh token to obtain a new one
without sending the user through authorization again:

```http
POST /oauth/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=refresh_token
&refresh_token=THE_REFRESH_TOKEN
&client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID
```

Refresh tokens rotate on use — the previous one is invalidated, so a leaked refresh token
has a narrow window.

## Verifying tokens offline

Access tokens are signed JWTs. Your service can verify them without calling NeoWorks by
fetching the public keys from the JWKS endpoint and caching them:

```
GET /oauth/.well-known/jwks.json
```

### What to check

- **Signature** against the JWKS public key.
- **`exp`** — the token has not expired.
- **`aud`** — the token was issued for your client.
- **`scope`** — the action you're about to perform is covered.

## The Vault sandbox

For end-to-end-encrypted data, crypto runs inside a cross-origin Vault iframe served by
the `oauth` service. Your app requests encrypt/decrypt operations over `postMessage` and
never receives raw key material — keys never leave the sandbox origin.
